Some spoilers (about potions and altars) follow...

Someone recently posted a spoiler about wielding potions, noting that 
wielding a potion of blindess would make you blind, for instance.

That's a horrible addition to the game.  First, it doesn't make any
sense.  Why should wielding a potion give you the effects of the
potion?  Second, it isn't the kind of thing anyone is likely to try
unless they are purposely trying all kinds of nonsensical things.

Doors are another bad addition to the game.  They add little and
impose an unneeded strain on the player.  It doesn't take much effort
to realize that doors would be much improved if (a) they displayed
differently in different states, so that the player doesn't have to
constantly shift his gaze to the message line and (b) if running into
doors attempted to open them, rather than giving some stupid message.

Contrast this with another addition to 3.0.  If you drop an item on an
altar, you identify it as cursed, non-cursed or blessed.  That's a
*good* addition to the game.  It makes sense in the game universe and
it is the kind of thing that a player might do or discover accidently.

There isn't any trick to make a game arbitrarily hard by putting in
all kinds of non-intuitive or random puzzles.  Nethack is beginning to
look more and more like a badly-written Adventure game, with winning
being dependent on a handful of tricks that can only be discovered
through random experimentation.

I don't think the current implementation of Nethack is all bad.  In
many places it is extremely clever and well-written.  (Check out where
you are wearing your rings while polymorphed!)  But some restraint
needs to be used in adding gimmicks to the game.  Too many random
bells and whistles detract from the basic appeal of the game - which
is, apologists notwithstanding - the good old dungeon crawl, the
"hjkl" keys, bumping into monsters and running down hallways.

